<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Work song · Dance · date unknown · Central &amp; East Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Sōran Bushi</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ja">ソーラン節</bdi> / <bdi lang="ja-Latn">Sōran Bushi</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Japanese sea shanty first sung by Hokkaido fishermen, now danced in schools with moves of waves and nets.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Fishermen of Hokkaido, Japan</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Hokkaido, Japan</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Work song · Dance</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soran-bushi">Sōran Bushi</a>&quot; is one of the best-known traditional songs and dances (min'yō) of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. It is a sea shanty, said to have been first sung by the fishermen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hokkaido">Hokkaido</a>. Shouted calls punctuate it at regular intervals; in the past such calls were used to spur the fishermen on as they worked.</p><p>The version most people know today is much more recent. In 1988 Takio Ito recorded &quot;Takio no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soran-bushi">Sōran Bushi</a>&quot;, a modernised version of the old song with a faster rhythm and more modern music and words. In 1991 Wakkanai Minami Junior High School created a song and dance called &quot;Nanchū Sōran&quot; on Ito's song and text, with choreography by the Butoh dancer Jushō Kasuga that acts out ocean waves, fishermen dragging nets, pulling ropes and lifting loads onto their shoulders.</p><p>The school received an honourable mention at the national convention of the Folk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and Dance Awards in 1992, and the next year the Grand Prix of the Prime Minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. The song and dance became known across the country, and the dance is now taught in many Japanese schools as part of the curriculum.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A fishermen's working song, its rhythm and calls keeping the crew together as they haul in their catch. The mood is vigorous and rousing.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords" data-say>The words of this song are in the public domain, but no free, reliable edition of them has been found yet to import them from, and the Institute never types a song&#8217;s words from memory. &#8220;What it says&#8221; above tells what they are about.</p></section>
</div>
